% The teaching authority of the Church claims unique standing to interpret human dignity through the imago Dei doctrine and to guide technological development through Catholic Social Doctrine. Sets normative frameworks for AI design that embed Catholic anthropology: person as relational, embodied, finite yet transcendent. Authority derives from lineage (apostolic succession, papal magisterium) and theological expertise. Enforcement is through moral suasion, institutional witness, and appeal to conscience of AI developers and policymakers.
narrative_ontology:constraint_stakeholder(human_dignity_ai_governance__magisterial_integralist_reading, catholic_magisterium, agenda_setter,
    institutional, civilizational, analytical, global).

% Workers, the poor, migrants, the elderly, and those without technical literacy bear the extractive burdens of AI systems most acutely — algorithmic discrimination in hiring, surveillance in labor management, social isolation through automation, elder abandonment. The Magisterial reading protects their dignity as irreducible, requiring AI design to respect relational and embodied human goods. Their exit options are severely constrained; they depend on institutional advocacy and legal safeguards.
narrative_ontology:constraint_stakeholder(human_dignity_ai_governance__magisterial_integralist_reading, vulnerable_populations, beneficiary,
    powerless, biographical, trapped, global).

% Face displacement through automation and algorithmic management systems that treat labor as a cost to be minimized rather than as participation in human dignity and community contribution. The Magisterial reading affirms the right to meaningful work, just wages, and dignity in labor — directly contradicting purely efficiency-driven AI development. Their advocacy capacity is higher than powerless populations but remains constrained by capital mobility and technical opacity.
narrative_ontology:constraint_stakeholder(human_dignity_ai_governance__magisterial_integralist_reading, workers, beneficiary,
    moderate, biographical, constrained, national).

% Experience erosion of familial bonds through surveillance technology, social media algorithms designed to maximize engagement over relational health, and economic pressures from AI-driven labor displacement. The Magisterial reading emphasizes the family as the foundational social unit and site of human flourishing; it requires AI governance to protect family stability and intergenerational transmission of values.
narrative_ontology:constraint_stakeholder(human_dignity_ai_governance__magisterial_integralist_reading, families, beneficiary,
    moderate, generational, constrained, national).

% Religious communities (Catholic parishes, dioceses, and international networks) benefit from the Magisterial reading's authority claim — they become central institutional voices in AI governance discourse. Catholic Social Teaching provides a framework for collective witness and advocacy. They have some exit options through ecumenical or secular alliances but derive much of their institutional standing from this particular theological claim.
