% engine consumes only the two atoms below; the founding-problem narrative
% is NEVER consumed as a claim, mismatch-consumer only, OQ-83 R5) ---
% COORDINATION_FUNCTION: Solves the problem of how to design AI systems that embed respect for human dignity as irreducible, relational, and transcendent — rather than as instrumental efficiency or as a platform for posthuman enhancement. Provides a shared anthropological framework (imago Dei, Catholic Social Doctrine) that coordinates the values embedded in AI architecture, labor policy, family protection, and governance structures.
% TRANSFER_FUNCTION: Transfers agenda-setting authority over AI design from technological experts and market actors to the Magisterium and faith-aligned institutions. Moves compliance costs from corporations to developers (design constraints, ethical review, slowdown of innovation). Extracts recognition and institutional authority for Catholic teaching as a binding voice in technology governance.
% ABSENT_VOICES: Secular humanists, techno-optimists, and proponents of democratic pluralism who would argue that no single theological tradition should have authority over AI governance. Transhumanist thinkers and enhancement advocates who view the Magisterial reading's anthropology as a constraint on human flourishing. Voices of technologically displaced workers from regions without Catholic institutional infrastructure. Engineers and developers in non-Catholic-majority jurisdictions who experience this reading's influence as imposed without their consent.
% DISAPPEARANCE_RATIONALE: If the Magisterial integralist reading vanished, AI development would accelerate toward efficiency-maximizing and enhancement-enabling designs; labor regulation would relax; family-protective design mandates would lift; governance authority would consolidate in secular technical and market institutions. Catholic institutions would lose their distinctive voice in technology ethics. Vulnerable populations would lose an organized institutional advocate rooted in dignity claims beyond utility. The anthropological grounding of AI design would shift from theological transcendence to secular capability maximization.
% FOUNDING_PROBLEM: Early AI development (2010s–2020s) operated under a technocratic, predominantly secular framework that treated AI as a neutral tool for whatever goals its builders chose. This framework systematized discrimination, displaced workers, eroded family bonds, and subordinated human dignity to efficiency metrics. Catholic moral theology observed that no technology is neutral; all embeds anthropological claims. The founding problem was: how can the Church's witness to human dignity as imago Dei shape AI development to protect vulnerable populations and preserve relational human goods?
% FOUNDING_PROBLEM_CORROBORATION: The Magisterium attests the founding problem is live through Vatican documents (Fratelli Tutti, 2020; Caritas in Veritate on technology; papal addresses on AI). Independent verification comes from: labor economists documenting AI-driven displacement without social protection (Acemoglu & Johnson, MIT); family researchers documenting social-media effects on relational health (Boyd, Turkle, Dunn); bioethicists from secular traditions (Floridi, Vallor) corroborating that AI systems do embed anthropological claims. The founding problem status is contested only by techno-optimists and pure-efficiency advocates; independent observers from outside the benefiting parties (vulnerable populations, faith communities) attest that the technological-neutrality framework has failed to protect human dignity.
narrative_ontology:disappearance_verdict(human_dignity_ai_governance__magisterial_integralist_reading, world_rearranges).
narrative_ontology:founding_problem_status(human_dignity_ai_governance__magisterial_integralist_reading, live).

